ardeen muhammad was once a successful  farmer and trader  but he's been living like this for more  than a decade  this makeshift structure is why he's  trying to raise a new family after boko  haram violence uprooted his life he says  his chain of businesses houses and farms  all perished in a single attack in 2010.  i had more houses more farms more shops  and warehouses and kamala than everyone  else look at me now i can't even fend  for myself even the room i live in was  given to me rent-free by a former  business partner but with peace  returning i look forward to going back  to rebuild  italian  the boko haram conflict in northeastern  nigeria has displaced more than two and  a half million people forcing them into  camps  now the government wants to shut down  these camps by the end of this year as  reconstruction gathers momentum  this small pile of rubble is what is  left of the former school and home of  muhammad yusuf the founder of boko haram  this is why the group's ideology was  nurtured nigerian security forces  demolished the structures shortly after  the rebellion was put down in 2009 but  the movement grew even bigger and  continues to kill people and destroy  property in northern nigeria  an estimated 300 000 people have been  killed  six billion dollars worth of property  and facilities lie in ruins in borno  state alone  people have completely lost their means  of livelihood  many of our educational facilities were  destroyed  about 5000 classrooms are destroyed  about 250 health centers are destroyed  about  800 municipal buildings were destroyed  comprising of  traditional rulers  homes  police station local government  secretariats among others as thousands  returned to their old villages this is  what they find  new houses are coming up in communities  where 900 000 homes were destroyed  in a direct challenge to boko haram  whose ideologies against western  education and values hundreds of schools  raised down by the armed group are being  r